   "html_text": "In 1846, Feuerbach published a number of “fragments” from his unpublished papers intended to document the course of his philosophical development. One of these fragments, entitled “Doubt”, and dated 1827–28 (which is the time when Feuerbach was writing his doctoral dissertation) seems already to anticipate Feuerbach’s later critique of Hegel. In this fragment, we find him questioning the transition from the first to the second part of Hegel’s tripartite philosophical system, i.e., the transition",
   "md_text": "In 1846, Feuerbach published a number of “fragments” from his unpublished papers intended to document the course of his philosophical development. One of these fragments, entitled “Doubt”, and dated 1827–28 (which is the time when Feuerbach was writing his doctoral dissertation) seems already to anticipate Feuerbach’s later critique of Hegel. In this fragment, we find him questioning the transition from the first to the second part of Hegel’s tripartite philosophical system, i.e., the transition"
